  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,3)$ and the line $y=-2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/67f63247ff1f91fae17f257512849ac2415a3894d57475d1e8a7afee1094df31.png)\n\nSince the line $y=-2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-3,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $-2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|3-(-2)|=5.\n$$",
